.TH STTY 1
.SH NAME
stty \- set terminal options
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "stty [ option ... ]"
.br
.B "STTY [ option ... ]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stty
sets certain I/O options on the current output terminal.
With no argument, it reports the current settings of the options.
.PP
.B STTY
is identical to
.BR stty ,
but given an upper-case name so that upper-case-only terminals can also set the terminal modes.
.PP
.B stty
can be used to set the terminal modes on any device merely by redirecting the standard output to that device through the shell `>' construction.
.PP
If your terminal has been placed into certain non-standard modes, you will have to use line-feeds (^J) instead of carriage returns at the end of lines.
.PP
The option strings are selected from the following set:
.TP 10
.B scroll
stop every 20 lines of screen output until a ^Q or any other character is typed; useful for keeping text from scrolling off CRT screens
.TP
.BR \- scroll
disable
.B scroll
option
.TP
.B crt
make `delete' echo as a backspace-space-backspace to erase a character from a CRT screen
.TP
.BR \- crt
make `delete' echo a `<' to indicate a deleted character, appropriate for hard-copy terminals
.TP
.B raw
raw mode input (no erase, kill, interrupt, quit, EOT; parity bit passed back)
.TP
.BR \- raw
negate raw mode
.TP
.B cooked
same as
.BR \- raw
.TP
.B cbreak
make each character available to
.BR read (2)
as received; no erase and kill
.TP
.BR \- cbreak
make characters available to
.B read
only when newline is received
.TP
.BR \- nl
allow carriage return for newline, and output CR\-LF for carriage return or newline
.TP
.B nl
accept only newline to end lines
.TP
.B echo
echo back every character typed
.TP
.BR \- echo
do not echo characters
.TP
.B lcase
map upper case to lower case
.TP
.BR \- lcase
do not map case
.TP
.BR \- tabs
replace tabs by spaces when printing
.TP
.B tabs
preserve tabs
.TP
.B ek
reset erase and kill characters back to normal (``DELETE key'' and ``^U'', respectively)
.TP
.BI erase " c"
set erase character to
.IR c .
.I c
can be of the form ``X'' which is interpreted as a `control\-X'.
.TP
.BI kill " c"
set kill character to
.IR c .
``X'' works here also.
.TP
.B even
allow even parity
.TP
.BR \- even
disallow even parity
.TP
.B odd
allow odd parity
.TP
.BR \- odd
disallow odd parity
.IP
(parity checking is not supported on all interfaces)
.TP
.B hup
hang up dataphone on last close.
.TP
.BR \- hup
do not hang up dataphone on last close.
.IP
(this option is not supported on all interfaces)
.TP
.B 0
hang up phone line immediately
.TP
.B "50 75 110 134 150 200 300 600 1200 1800 2400 4800 9600 exta extb"
Set terminal baud rate to the number given, if possible.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set current terminal modes appropriate to normal CRT usage, with the terminal output stopping every 20 lines waiting for a ^Q.
.RS
.nf
stty cooked echo crt \-nl scroll
.fi
.RE
Set terminal
.I tty02
to
.I 300
baud:
.RS
.nf
stty 300 > /dev/tty02
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ioctl(2)
.sp
